cc7a013e8726ab3b09289e69d8.png)\n\nConsider the function $y=f(x)$ plotted above. For what values of $x$ does the function $y=f(x)$ have a point discontinuity?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040337/state.json" "file_sha256" "4370e383ebb56ec5bdf412eccb3a5b175804e36f1d6b6a69fab340f9fbb81662"} {"question" "q-48161" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The function has a discontinuity at $x=3.$ Checking our continuity conditions, we have:\n\n- $f(3)=1$ exists $\\,✓$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to 3}f(x)=0$ exists $\\,✓$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to 3}f(x)\\ne f(3)\\,\\times $\n\nSo it's the third condition that failed, which tells us that $x=3$ is a removable discontinuity.\n\nNote that the function also has a discontinuity at $x=-3,$ but it is not a point discontinuity. Checking the conditions, we find that\n\n- $f(-3)$ does not exist. $\\,\\,\\times $\n\nSo this time, the first condition failed, and therefore $x=-3$ is not a point discontinuity.\n\nConsequently, the correct answer is $x=3$ only." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040337/state.json" "file_sha256" "4370e383ebb56ec5bdf412eccb3a5b175804e36f1d6b6a69fab340f9fbb81662"} {"question" "q-48161" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040337/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f3fa4efa22fce0b97d0b77d07860e5b5303e13e3efeeaf68824fdb791435e57c"} {"question" "q-48161" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040337/state.json" "file_sha256" "4370e383ebb56ec5bdf412eccb3a5b175804e36f1d6b6a69fab340f9fbb81662"} {"question" "q-48161" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$x=-3$ and $x=3$ only"] ["text" "$x=-3$ only"] ["text" "$x=3$ and $x=2$ only"] ["text" "$x=3$ only"] ["text" "$x=\\pm 3$ and $x=2$ only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040337/state.json" "file_sha256" "4370e383ebb56ec5bdf412eccb3a5b175804e36f1d6b6a69fab340f9fbb81662"} {"question" "q-48161" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$x=3$ only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040337/state.json" "file_sha256" "4370e383ebb56ec5bdf412eccb3a5b175804e36f1d6b6a69fab340f9fbb81662"} {"question" "q-104415" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040337/assets/8050047cf4381ef77d5569ac23b2adaa6b8484f5be672fd9a52bf011beeb39e8.png)\n\nConsider the function $y=f(x)$ plotted above. For what values of $x$ does the function $y=f(x)$ have a removable discontinuity?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040337/state.json" "file_sha256" "4370e383ebb56ec5bdf412eccb3a5b175804e36f1d6b6a69fab340f9fbb81662"} {"question" "q-104415" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The function has a discontinuity at $x=-1.$ Checking our continuity conditions, we have:\n\n- $f(-1)=2$ exists $\\,✓$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to (-1)}f(x)=-3$ exists $\\,✓$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to (-1)}f(x)\\ne f(-1)\\,\\times $\n\nSo it's the third condition that failed, which tells us that $x=-1$ is a removable discontinuity.\n\nThe function also has a discontinuity at $x=3.$ Checking our continuity conditions, we have:\n\n- $f(3)=-2$ exists $\\,✓$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to 3}f(x)=1$ exists $\\,✓$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to 3}f(x)\\ne f(3)\\,\\times $\n\nSo it's the third condition that failed, which tells us that $x=3$ is also a removable discontinuity.\n\nFinally,  the function has a discontinuity at $x=5.$ Checking our continuity conditions, we have:\n\n- $f(5)$ does not exists $\\,\\times $\n\nSo it's the first condition that failed, which tells us that $x=5$ is not a removable discontinuity.\n\nConsequently, the correct answer is $x=-1$ and $x=3$ only." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040337/state.json" "file_sha256" "4370e383ebb56ec5bdf412eccb3a5b175804e36f1d6b6a69fab340f9fbb81662"} {"question" "q-104415" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040337/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f3fa4efa22fce0b97d0b77d07860e5b5303e13e3efeeaf68824fdb791435e57c"} {"question" "q-104415" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040337/state.json" "file_sha256" "4370e383ebb56ec5bdf412eccb3a5b175804e36f1d6b6a69fab340f9fbb81662"} {"question" "q-104415" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$x=-1$ and $x=3$ only"] ["text" "$x=-1$ and $x=5$ only"] ["text" "$x=-1$ only"] ["text" "$x=3$ and $x=5$ only"] ["text" "$x=3$ only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040337/state.json" "file_sha256" "4370e383ebb56ec5bdf412eccb3a5b175804e36f1d6b6a69fab340f9fbb81662"} {"question" "q-104415" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$x=-1$ and $x=3$ only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040337/state.json" "file_sha256" "4370e383ebb56ec5bdf412eccb3a5b175804e36f1d6b6a69fab340f9fbb81662"} {"question" "q-48125" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given the functions $\\,f(x),\\,g(x)\\,$ and $\\,h(x)\\,$ below, which of them has a point discontinuity at $x=0?$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={\\begin{aligned}{2}^{x}+3 & x<0 \\\\ 4, & x=0 \\\\ 3x+4, & x>0\\end{aligned} \\\\  \\\\ g(x) & ={\\begin{aligned}2(x+1), & x<0 \\\\ 0, & x=0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+2, & x>0\\end{aligned} \\\\  \\\\ h(x) & ={\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}+1, & x\\le 0 \\\\ {x}^{5}+12x+1, & x>0\\end{aligned}\\text{ }\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040337/state.json" "file_sha256" "4370e383ebb56ec5bdf412eccb3a5b175804e36f1d6b6a69fab340f9fbb81662"} {"question" "q-48125" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's check each function, in turn:\n\nLet's check our continuity conditions for $f(x)$ at $x=0:$\n\n- $f(0)=4$ exists $✓$\n\n- The left and right-sided limits are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{-}}f(x) & ={2}^{0}+3=4, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{+}}f(x) & =3(0)+4=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the left and right-sided limits are equal, we conclude\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 0}f(x)=4.\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to 0}f(x)=f(0)$ $\\,\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the function $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=0.$\n\nNow, let's check our continuity conditions for $g(x)$ at $x=0:$\n\n- $g(0)=0$ exists $✓$\n\n- The left and right-sided limits are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{-}}g(x) & =2(0+1)=2, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{+}}g(x) & ={0}^{2}+2=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the left and right-sided limits are equal, we conclude\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 0}g(x)=2.\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to 0}g(x)\\ne g(0)$ $\\,\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the function $g(x)$ is not continuous at $x=0.$ It's the third condition that failed, which tells us that $x=0$ is a point discontinuity. \n\nFinally, let's check our continuity conditions for $h(x)$ at $x=0:$\n\n- $h(0)=2(0)^{2}+1=1$ exists $✓$\n\n- The left and right-sided limits are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{-}}h(x) & =2(0)^{2}+1=1, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{+}}h(x) & =(0)^{5}+12(0)+1=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the left and right-sided limits are equal, we conclude\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 0}h(x)=1.\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to 0}h(x)=h(0)$ $\\,\\,✓$\n\nSo, the function $h(x)$ is continuous at $x=0.$\n\nTherefore, only $g(x)$ has a point discontinuity at $x=0.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040337/state.json" "file_sha256" "4370e383ebb56ec5bdf412eccb3a5b175804e36f1d6b6a69fab340f9fbb81662"} {"question" "q-48125" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040337/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f3fa4efa22fce0b97d0b77d07860e5b5303e13e3efeeaf68824fdb791435e57c"} {"question" "q-48125" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040337/state.json" "file_sha256" "4370e383ebb56ec5bdf412eccb3a5b175804e36f1d6b6a69fab340f9fbb81662"} {"question" "q-48125" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$f(x)$ and $h(x)$ only"] ["text" "$f(x)$ only"] ["text" "$g(x)$ and $h(x)$ only"] ["text" "$g(x)$ only"] ["text" "$h(x)$ only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040337/state.json" "file_sha256" "4370e383ebb56ec5bdf412eccb3a5b175804e36f1d6b6a69fab340f9fbb81662"} {"question" "q-48125" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$g(x)$ only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040337/state.json" "file_sha256" "4370e383ebb56ec5bdf412eccb3a5b175804e36f1d6b6a69fab340f9fbb81662"} {"question" "q-71179" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(x) & ={\\begin{aligned}3x, & x\\ne 4 \\\\ 13, & x=4,\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to {4}^{+}}g(x)={lim}_{x\\to {4}^{-}}g(x)$\n- ${lim}_{x\\to 4}g(x)=g(4)$\n- $g(x)$ has a removable discontinuity at $x=4$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040337/state.json" "file_sha256" "4370e383ebb56ec5bdf412eccb3a5b175804e36f1d6b6a69fab340f9fbb81662"} {"question" "q-71179" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's check our continuity conditions:\n\n- $g(4)=13$ exists$\\,\\,✓$\n\n- The left and right-sided limits are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,{4}^{-}}g(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\,{4}^{-}}3x=12, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to \\,{4}^{+}}g(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\,{4}^{+}}3x=12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the left and right-sided limits are equal, we conclude that statement I is true and\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 4}g(x)=12.\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\n- The value of the limit is ${lim}_{x\\to 4}g(x)=12,$ while the value of the function is $g(4)=13.$ So, statement II is false and\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 4}g(x)\\ne g(4)\\,\\,\\times \n$$\n\nSince the third condition failed, the function has a removable discontinuity at $x=4,$ and statement III is true.\n\nIn conclusion, only statements I and III are true." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040337/state.json" "file_sha256" "4370e383ebb56ec5bdf412eccb3a5b175804e36f1d6b6a69fab340f9fbb81662"} {"question" "q-71179" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040337/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f3fa4efa22fce0b97d0b77d07860e5b5303e13e3efeeaf68824fdb791435e57c"} {"question" "q-71179" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040337/state.json" "file_sha256" "4370e383ebb56ec5bdf412eccb3a5b175804e36f1d6b6a69fab340f9fbb81662"} {"question" "q-71179" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "III only"] ["text" "None"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040337/state.json" "file_sha256" "4370e383ebb56ec5bdf412eccb3a5b175804e36f1d6b6a69fab340f9fbb81662"} {"question" "q-71179" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and III only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040337/state.json" "file_sha256" "4370e383ebb56ec5bdf412eccb3a5b175804e36f1d6b6a69fab340f9fbb81662"} {"question" "q-104575" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(x) & ={\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-34, & x<-3 \\\\ -25, & x=-3 \\\\ {x}^{3}+2, & x>-3\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to (-3)^{+}}g(x)={lim}_{x\\to (-3)^{-}}g(x)$\n- ${lim}_{x\\to (-3)}g(x)=g(-3)$\n- $g(x)$ has a removable discontinuity at $x=-3$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040337/state.json" "file_sha256" "4370e383ebb56ec5bdf412eccb3a5b175804e36f1d6b6a69fab340f9fbb81662"} {"question" "q-104575" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's check our continuity conditions:\n\n- $g(-3)=-25$ exists $\\,✓$\n\n- The left and right-sided limits are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,(-3)^{-}}g(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\,(-3)^{-}}({x}^{2}-34)=(-3)^{2}-34=-25, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to \\,(-3)^{+}}g(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\,(-3)^{+}}({x}^{3}+2)=(-3)^{3}+2=-25.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the left and right-sided limits are equal, we conclude that statement I is true and\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (-3)}g(x)=-25.\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\n- The value of the limit is ${lim}_{x\\to (-3)}g(x)=-25,$ and the value of the function is $g(-3)=-25.$ So, statement II is true and\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (-3)}g(x)=g(-3)\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\nSo, the function is continuous at $x=-3,$ and statement III is false.\n\nIn conclusion, only statements I and II are true." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040337/state.json" "file_sha256" "4370e383ebb56ec5bdf412eccb3a5b175804e36f1d6b6a69fab340f9fbb81662"} {"question" "q-104575" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040337/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f3fa4efa22fce0b97d0b77d07860e5b5303e13e3efeeaf68824fdb791435e57c"} {"question" "q-104575" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040337/state.json" "file_sha256" "4370e383ebb56ec5bdf412eccb3a5b175804e36f1d6b6a69fab340f9fbb81662"} {"question" "q-104575" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "III only"] ["text" "None"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040337/state.json" "file_sha256" "4370e383ebb56ec5bdf412eccb3a5b175804e36f1d6b6a69fab340f9fbb81662"} {"question" "q-104575" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and II only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040337/state.json" "file_sha256" "4370e383ebb56ec5bdf412eccb3a5b175804e36f1d6b6a69fab340f9fbb81662"} {"question" "q-71181" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(x) & ={\\begin{aligned}5x-5, & x<1 \\\\ 0, & x=1 \\\\ {x}^{3}-1, & x>1\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}g(x)={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}g(x)$\n- ${lim}_{x\\to 1}g(x)=g(1)$\n- $g(x)$ has a removable discontinuity at $x=1$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040337/state.json" "file_sha256" "4370e383ebb56ec5bdf412eccb3a5b175804e36f1d6b6a69fab340f9fbb81662"} {"question" "q-71181" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's check our continuity conditions:\n\n- $g(1)=0$ exists $\\,✓$\n\n- The left and right-sided limits are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}g(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}(5x-5)=5(1)-5=0, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{+}}g(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{+}}({x}^{3}-1)={1}^{3}-1=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the left and right-sided limits are equal, we conclude that statement I is true and\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 1}g(x)=0.\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\n- The value of the limit is ${lim}_{x\\to 1}g(x)=0,$ and the value of the function is $g(1)=0.$ So, statement II is true and\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 1}g(x)=g(1)\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\nSo, the function is continuous at $x=0,$ and statement III is false.\n\nIn conclusion, only statements I and II are true." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040337/state.json" "file_sha256" "4370e383ebb56ec5bdf412eccb3a5b175804e36f1d6b6a69fab340f9fbb81662"} {"question" "q-71181" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040337/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f3fa4efa22fce0b97d0b77d07860e5b5303e13e3efeeaf68824fdb791435e57c"} {"question" "q-71181" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040337/state.json" "file_sha256" "4370e383ebb56ec5bdf412eccb3a5b175804e36f1d6b6a69fab340f9fbb81662"} {"question" "q-71181" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "III only"] ["text" "None"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040337/state.json" "file_sha256" "4370e383ebb56ec5bdf412eccb3a5b175804e36f1d6b6a69fab340f9fbb81662"} {"question" "q-71181" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and II only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040337/state.json" "file_sha256" "4370e383ebb56ec5bdf412eccb3a5b175804e36f1d6b6a69fab340f9fbb81662"} {"question" "q-104572" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{x+11}, & x<5 \\\\ 12, & x=5 \\\\ {e}^{5/x}, & x>5\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements is true?\n\n- $f(5)$ exists\n- ${lim}_{x\\to {5}^{+}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to {5}^{-}}f(x)$\n- $f(x)$ has a removable discontinuity at $x=5$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040337/state.json" "file_sha256" "4370e383ebb56ec5bdf412eccb3a5b175804e36f1d6b6a69fab340f9fbb81662"} {"question" "q-104572" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's check our continuity conditions:\n\n- $f(5)=12$ exists $\\,\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, statement I is true.\n\n- The left and right-sided limits are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,{5}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\,{5}^{-}}\\sqrt{x+11}=4, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to \\,{5}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\,{5}^{+}}{e}^{5/x}=e\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that statement II is false and\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {5}^{+}}f(x)\\ne {lim}_{x\\to {5}^{-}}f(x).\\,\\,\\times \n$$\n\nSince the second condition failed, $x=5$ is not a removable discontinuity. So, statement III is false.\n\nIn conclusion, only statement I is true." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040337/state.json" "file_sha256" "4370e383ebb56ec5bdf412eccb3a5b175804e36f1d6b6a69fab340f9fbb81662"} {"question" "q-104572" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040337/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f3fa4efa22fce0b97d0b77d07860e5b5303e13e3efeeaf68824fdb791435e57c"} {"question" "q-104572" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040337/state.json" "file_sha256" "4370e383ebb56ec5bdf412eccb3a5b175804e36f1d6b6a69fab340f9fbb81662"} {"question" "q-104572" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "I, II, and III"] ["text" "II only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040337/state.json" "file_sha256" "4370e383ebb56ec5bdf412eccb3a5b175804e36f1d6b6a69fab340f9fbb81662"} {"question" "q-104572" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040337/state.json" "file_sha256" "4370e383ebb56ec5bdf412eccb3a5b175804e36f1d6b6a69fab340f9fbb81662"} {"question" "e-3580" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "For which points does the function $y=f(x)$ below have a point discontinuity?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040337/assets/10ba0496a4dc42579bb95d371a65d808edc0cc5a5653ab3e23d4fe85adf90518.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040337/example-3580.json" "file_sha256" "3d2a52408aea3df6aeda30b162a1a4075f8cae436884769f609fabf33122f3bd"} {"question" "e-3580" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The function has a discontinuity at $x=2.$ Checking our continuity conditions, we have\n\n- $f(2)= 2$ exists $\\,\\,{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}$\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to 2}f(x)= 1$ exists $\\,\\,{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}$\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to 2}f(x) \\neq f(2)$ $\\,\\,{\\color{red}{\\times}}$\n\nSo it's the third condition that failed, which tells us that $x=2$ is a point discontinuity." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040337/example-3580.json" "file_sha256" "3d2a52408aea3df6aeda30b162a1a4075f8cae436884769f609fabf33122f3bd"} {"question" "e-3579" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "For which points does the function $y=f(x)$ below have a removable discontinuity?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040337/assets/159566da49ef22ea1aae7800355949ae3cf02dac6a6a02e0a8bc344db176a75d.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040337/example-3579.json" "file_sha256" "66e3dbcfb639dbffb48f7f5ec210c624d63acff7ad4429bbceaebb849d736e74"} {"question" "e-3579" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The function has a discontinuity at $x=-3.$ Checking our continuity conditions, we have\n\n- $f(-3)= 2$ exists $\\,\\,{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}$\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to -3}f(x)= -2$ exists $\\,\\,{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}$\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to -3}f(x) \\neq f(3)$ $\\,\\,{\\color{red}{\\times}}$\n\nSo it's the third condition that failed, which tells us that $x=-3$ is a removable discontinuity. A similar argument applies for $x=3$, which is also a removable discontinuity. So, the function has removable discontinuities at $x=-3, 3.$ \n\nNote that the function also has a discontinuity at $x=0,$ but it is not a removable discontinuity. Checking the conditions, we find that\n\n- $f(0)= -2$ exists $\\,\\,{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}$\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to 0}f(x)$ does not exist $\\,\\,{\\color{red}{\\times}}$\n\nSo this time, the second condition failed, and therefore $x=0$ is not a removable discontinuity." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040337/example-3579.json" "file_sha256" "66e3dbcfb639dbffb48f7f5ec210c624d63acff7ad4429bbceaebb849d736e74"} {"question" "e-3581" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given the function $f(x),$ defined by\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x+3, & x<0 \\\\ 0, & x=0 \\\\ 3-x, & x>0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\lim\\limits_{x\\to 0^+} f(x)=\\lim\\limits_{x\\to 0^-} f(x)$\n\n- $\\lim\\limits_{x\\to 0} f(x)=f(0)$\n\n- $f(x)$ has a removable discontinuity at $x=0$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040337/example-3581.json" "file_sha256" "45172e567052f8433237d9f88f139845d48f36a4dd33eb781d349da4f6e8a289"} {"question" "e-3581" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's check our continuity conditions:\n\n- $f(0)=0$ exists $\\,\\,{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}$\n\n- The left and right-sided limits are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}(x+3)=3, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}(3-x)=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the left and right-sided limits are equal, we conclude that statement I is true and\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\lim\\limits_{x\\to 0} f(x) = 3. \\,\\,{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}\n$$\n\n- The value of the limit is $\\displaystyle\\lim\\limits_{x\\to 0} f(x) = 3,$ while the value of the function is $f(0)=0.$ So, statement II is false and\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\lim\\limits_{x\\to 0} f(x)\\neq f(0)\\,\\,{\\color{red}{\\times}}\n$$\n\nSince the third condition failed, the function has a removable discontinuity at $x=0,$ and statement III is true.\n\nIn conclusion, only statements I and III are true." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040337/example-3581.json" "file_sha256" "45172e567052f8433237d9f88f139845d48f36a4dd33eb781d349da4f6e8a289"}] "usage" {"e-3579" [] "q-71181" [] "e-3581" [] "q-48115" [] "e-3580" []} "basis" 1431 "retractions" [[17592186129733 :question/problem "Which of the following functions has a removable discontinuity at $x = 3$?"] [17592186129773 :question/problem "Given that\n$g(x)|= {5x - 5,\\begin{vmatrix}x < 1 \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}x = 1; x^{3} - 1, \\mid x > 1$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $lim_(x → 1^{+})g(x) = lim_(x → 1^{-})g(x)$\n2. $lim_(x → 1)g(x) = g(1)$\n3. $g(x)$ has a removable discontinuity at $x = 1$"] [17592186128836 :question/problem "For which points does the function $y=f(x)$ below have a point discontinuity?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2002/Source/Images/10672c90c91cf5d9de3a618d768abfd2.png)"] [17592186128836 :question/worked-solution "The function has a discontinuity at $x=2$. Checking our continuity conditions, we have\n\n- $f(2)= 2$ exists $\\,\\,{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}$\n- $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to 2}f(x)= 1$ exists $\\,\\,{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}$\n- $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to 2}f(x) \\neq f(2)$ $\\,\\,{\\color{red}{\\times}}$\n\nSo it's the third condition that failed, which tells us that $x=2$ is a point discontinuity."] [17592186128835 :question/problem "For which points does the function $y=f(x)$ below have a removable discontinuity?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2002/Source/Images/8abe220313dcb5097dcb66f6b13d2190.png)"] [17592186128835 :question/worked-solution "The function has a discontinuity at $x=-3$. Checking our continuity conditions, we have\n\n- $f(-3)= 2$ exists $\\,\\,{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}$\n- $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to -3}f(x)= -2$ exists $\\,\\,{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}$\n- $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to -3}f(x) \\neq f(3)$ $\\,\\,{\\color{red}{\\times}}$\n\nSo it's the third condition that failed, which tells us that $x=-3$ is a removable discontinuity. A similar argument applies for $x=3$, which is also a removable discontinuity. So, the function has removable discontinuities at $x=-3, 3$.\n\nNote that the function also has a discontinuity at $x=0$, but it is not a removable discontinuity. Checking the conditions, we find that\n\n- $f(0)= -2$ exists $\\,\\,{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}$\n- $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to 0}f(x)$ does not exist $\\,\\,{\\color{red}{\\times}}$\n\nSo this time, the second condition failed, and therefore $x=0$ is not a removable discontinuity."] [17592186128837 :question/problem "Given the function $f(x)$, defined by\n${x + 3,\\begin{vmatrix}x < 0 \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}x = 0; 3 - x, \\mid x > 0$,\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $\\lim\\limits_{x\\to 0^+} f(x)=\\lim\\limits_{x\\to 0^-} f(x)$\n2. $\\lim\\limits_{x\\to 0} f(x)=f(0)$\n3. $f(x)$ has a removable discontinuity at $x=0$"] [17592186128837 :question/worked-solution "Let's check our continuity conditions:\n\n- $f(0)=0$ exists $\\,\\,{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}$\n- The left and right-sided limits are\n$lim_(x → 0^{-})f(x) = lim_(x → 0^{-})(x + 3) = 3,; lim_(x → 0^{+})f(x) = lim_(x → 0^{+})(3 - x) = 3$.\nSince the left and right-sided limits are equal, we conclude that statement I is true and\n$\\displaystyle\\lim\\limits_{x\\to 0} f(x) = 3. \\,\\,{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}$\n- The value of the limit is $\\displaystyle\\lim\\limits_{x\\to 0} f(x) = 3$, while the value of the function is $f(0)=0$. So, statement II is false and\n$\\displaystyle \\lim\\limits_{x\\to 0} f(x)\\neq f(0)\\,\\,{\\color{red}{\\times}}$\n\nSince the third condition failed, the function has a removable discontinuity at $x=0$, and statement III is true.\n\nIn conclusion, only statements I and III are true."]] "immutable_answers" [17592186128193 17592186128194 17592186128195 17592186128196 17592186128197 17592186128433 17592186128434 17592186128435 17592186128436 17592186128437] "immutable_fields" [[17592186128192